# `xs[i]` past the end ends the run, and ends it the same way on both
# backends.
#
# Knife 1 of docs/operator-traits-design.md, and the half of it that could not
# live in index_ok.dawn: a program about the end of the run has no output
# after the failure to compare. So this one declares itself with the marker
# file `index_oob.exits-nonzero`, and what is checked is the stdout up to the
# failure (against .expect) plus the stderr and the exit code, compared
# between the two backends. See the header of run.sh.
#
# What this catches that .expect alone cannot: a knife 2 that routed `[]`
# somewhere with no bounds check would come back with `at`'s answer instead --
# `std/pvec.at` masks the index back onto a legal slot and hands over some
# other element (pvec.dawn:150-159 records that exact defect and why `at` is
# not public). That failure prints a number and exits 0. Here it prints the
# WRONG line below, and the exit check turns 1-against-0 into a red.
#
# The message itself is not pinned here -- the harness compares stderr between
# the backends and has no way to say what it should read. index_panic_text.dawn
# is where the text is written down.
use std/io

## The index goes through `!io` so nothing folds: comptime out-of-bounds is a
## compile error (`error: comptime: index 9 out of bounds for length 3`), which
## is a different claim, belongs to a checker test, and would stop this program
## from being built at all.
fn n(i: Int) -> Int !io = i

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = {
  let xs = [10, 20, 30]
  println("in range")
  println(to_string(xs[n(0)]))
  println(to_string(xs[n(2)]))
  println("past the end")
  let v = xs[n(3)]
  # Unreachable. A backend that answered instead of panicking says so here
  # rather than passing quietly.
  println("WRONG: the subscript answered " ++ to_string(v))
}
